stActionDate":"2026-07-22","latestActionText":"Ordered to be Reported (Amended) by the Yeas and Nays: 40 - 2.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Civil actions and liability","Government employee pay, benefits, personnel management","Health care costs and insurance","Judicial review and appeals","Prescription drugs","User charges and fees"],"sponsorBioguideId":"A000148","cosponsorCount":39,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/6610","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Pharmacy benefit managers, which this bill targets with new liability and judicial review, are typically part of or contracted by health insurers managing federal employee drug benefits."},{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill directly affects pharmacists and the terms under which they dispense prescription drugs, altering the contractual and fee structure in the federal employee drug supply chain."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"The bill creates new federal civil causes of action and judicial review procedures, which would generate litigation between pharmacists and PBMs."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would allow pharmacists to sue pharmacy benefit managers (PBMs) in federal court if they believe PBM fees or contract terms are unfair, and would let courts review and potentially overturn those fees. The bill applies specifically to prescription drug plans offered to federal employees and retirees. It creates a new legal pathway for pharmacists to challenge the business practices of the middlemen who manage drug benefits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who fills a prescription. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"title-only"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"H001086","score":0.05366184557252676},"overlapCount":40},"meaning":{"percentile":64.22148603880738,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.556343